#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31f3ead1-2549-5b61-baf9-0437ba28f09d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using the Squared Sine Variant of the Cosine Double-Angle Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c779221-1a96-5a0a-95ee-7ffce3e0d80e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361720} {:answer/id #uuid "c2b31554-f53c-5090-becd-84389075d104" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361721} {:answer/id #uuid "832ecd68-e9de-52d4-9dfa-2eb63d77df59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361722} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d2adff-a695-526c-a7a8-eaa4be5da50e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361723} {:answer/id #uuid "b316147c-c6ef-56b7-b960-a1ebd09d2874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b316147c-c6ef-56b7-b960-a1ebd09d2874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361724} :answer-field/id #uuid "95f1b6f1-7c30-5049-8e29-bdaab28db919" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "39c87398-5175-5dc1-8cc4-8632b2bd67dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-114703" :question/problem "$\\int ({sin}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)={sin}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({sin}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31f3ead1-2549-5b61-baf9-0437ba28f09d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using the Squared Sine Variant of the Cosine Double-Angle Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54a3427a-3ed4-5605-9c49-74f759bcf245" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288690} {:answer/id #uuid "f567a014-d677-5c69-85cf-f6da6014595b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54a3427a-3ed4-5605-9c49-74f759bcf245" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288690} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fcd1929-8fa5-5e11-9699-492206e5fe2a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6aaa3cde-2f29-5bb8-a80e-dee5f4f375ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-347169" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}({cos}^{2}⁡(3x)-4{sin}^{2}⁡(3x))\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall  ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ so letting $\\theta =3x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)-4{sin}^{2}⁡(3x) & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)-4{sin}^{2}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =1-5{sin}^{2}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =3x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x))={sin}^{2}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}(1-5{sin}^{2}⁡(3x))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}(1-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}(\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)-\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-\\frac{3}{2}x)\\,|}_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{5}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{3})-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{3}]-[\\frac{5}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}] \\\\  & =[0-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}]-[0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}] \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361726 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b466bd78-724b-51b2-8fee-7991bf7b60d2" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using the Squared Cosine Variant of the Cosine Double-Angle Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0781bb8-a903-5df4-b276-c5f220c5aafc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0781bb8-a903-5df4-b276-c5f220c5aafc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361728} :answer-field/id #uuid "f31cfdac-75c6-5bf0-a419-b8f355559f35" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d5e356de-7912-5bf7-8fed-9681512abebd" :question/math-academy-id "q-348755" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069587/assets/e0a3fb0f4fc900f6ef8ac041ded7553f7ac44cc09f946bc3ebfee8a3235e8e94.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=5{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+1,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=0,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(5{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nRecall  ${cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1,$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+1 & =3{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2({sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2x))+1 \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n2{cos}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1.\n$$\n\nHence, the area of the region is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(3{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1)+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\frac{9}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\frac{9}{2}x)\\,|}_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot 0)-(\\frac{3}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )+\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =(0+0)-(0-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "064d3c62-5cfc-502b-aba4-48e40042593f" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Expressions Using the Double-Angle Formula for Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "842d857b-31f1-5712-a3a3-66b676c8fedd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361731} {:answer/id #uuid "ffbdfe91-1ce2-5b96-b7da-e9d6c5723d15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361732} {:answer/id #uuid "a34aa9e5-3650-562d-949b-cdba07b21c45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361733} {:answer/id #uuid "c31474a9-e897-5e21-9dca-6d82f023f9ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361734} {:answer/id #uuid "bfcf3d1a-f70c-51a3-aea0-17346305b26d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfcf3d1a-f70c-51a3-aea0-17346305b26d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361735} :answer-field/id #uuid "7131b056-aaab-50e9-9652-3361506fe444" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d2caaa3-5720-58a5-a204-32175641ff19" :question/math-academy-id "q-74407" :question/problem "$\\int (3{x}^{2}+10{sin}^{2}⁡5x-10{cos}^{2}⁡5x)\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =5x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x={cos}^{2}⁡5x-{sin}^{2}⁡5x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3{x}^{2}+10{sin}^{2}⁡5x-10{cos}^{2}⁡5x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (3{x}^{2}-10({cos}^{2}⁡5x-{sin}^{2}⁡5x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (3{x}^{2}-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-10(\\frac{1}{10}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x)+C \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b466bd78-724b-51b2-8fee-7991bf7b60d2" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using the Squared Cosine Variant of the Cosine Double-Angle Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88eef018-64bb-5b92-9c2f-1ec72b23190b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361738} {:answer/id #uuid "0c9fa166-eeae-5071-b0a1-4bd3d34b9996" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361739} {:answer/id #uuid "b5e0e150-4f95-50ed-81b8-24f3f0d94910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361740} {:answer/id #uuid "3c91618c-4258-5a14-8cd8-01e1586709c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361741} {:answer/id #uuid "46b5ef69-26de-5cc2-a63e-ea09ebba0936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5e0e150-4f95-50ed-81b8-24f3f0d94910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361740} :answer-field/id #uuid "16d79c9f-2069-5cd9-b62f-5a63f18b029f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff8c9059-4ccd-5c34-b361-0250f8da7c82" :question/math-academy-id "q-74408" :question/problem "Calculate \n$\\int (5-8{cos}^{2}⁡2x)\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =2x$, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡2x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5-8{cos}^{2}⁡2x)\\text{d}x & =\\int [5-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int [5-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x-4(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+C \\\\  & =x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
